# Containerized / CI environments: Podman creates /run/.containerenv, Docker
# creates /.dockerenv (neither exists on a bare-metal host or VM). Inside a
# container, deploy the CLI dotfiles only and skip all host provisioning:
# package installs, /etc system config, GPG/GitHub/Tailscale auth, fonts,
# KDE/GNOME settings, and pro-audio realtime provisioning.
#
# distrobox and toolbox are the OPT-OUT: both bind-mount the host $HOME and both
# create /run/.toolboxenv (distrobox touches it for toolbx compatibility), so an
# apply inside one writes to the real host $HOME and must behave like the host —
# provision fully. Skip only a "real" container: a Podman/Docker marker present
# WITHOUT /run/.toolboxenv.
#
# That whole predicate is the `container` fact — declared in .chezmoidata/facts.yaml
# and resolved once in .chezmoitemplates/facts.tmpl, which is also what the guard
# partials and the installers read. The markers are no longer stat'd here: one
# machine, one answer.
{{- if $f.container }}
.chezmoiscripts/30-linux/*.sh
.chezmoiscripts/20-base/**
.chezmoiscripts/30-components/**
.chezmoiscripts/50-linux-kde/*.sh
.chezmoiscripts/50-linux-gnome/*.sh
.chezmoiscripts/10-auth/*.sh
.chezmoiscripts/80-keys/*.sh
.config/fontconfig
{{/* containers skip non-container desktop targets */}}
.local/share/applications/wezterm.desktop
# The ~/src garden is a host concept: the 80-keys GPG key import is skipped
# above so the encrypted project registry cannot be decrypted here, and the
# 90-src reconcile script (grow/bootstrap/aoe) would have nothing to provision
# and would hard-fail on the then-absent registry — skip both.
.chezmoiscripts/90-src/*.sh
.config/garden/garden.yaml
# Both of these resolve host-only vault references at RENDER time -- the
# KAccounts provider reads Google-KDE-OAuth and the Wi-Fi tool reads the
# per-network items, all of which live in the vault a worker Connect token
# cannot read. A worker apply would demand a vault outside its token's scope
# and fail. Neither has meaning in a container anyway: one is a desktop
# online-accounts provider, the other is host network provisioning.
.local/share/accounts/providers/google.provider
.local/share/chezmoi-command-sources/import-wifi-1password
# Registry auth is a HOST concept. A worker pulls no images -- kubelet does that
# with the cluster's own imagePullSecrets -- so this file has no consumer in a
# pod, and it is the one target that composes a base64 `user:PAT` from four
# op:// reads. Excluding it is simpler and safer than gating it: there is no
# runtime apply that should ever write it.
.config/containers

# The rest of the worker trim (R13). These are whole-file targets with no owning
# entry in commands.yaml or agents.yaml, so a per-entry policy has nothing to
# attach to and the path is the only place the decision can live. Everything that
# DOES have an owning entry is declared there instead -- see
# .chezmoitemplates/ships-in-container.tmpl.
#
# Desktop applications. A pod has no session, no display and no audio server.
.config/1Password
.config/VSCodium
.config/autostart
.config/fcitx5
.config/ghostty
.config/wezterm
.config/wireplumber
.local/share/applications
.local/share/accounts
# A pod has no systemd, so neither of these is ever loaded. Note the consequence:
# DOCKER_HOST from environment.d/65-containers.conf is NOT set in a worker.
.config/systemd
.config/environment.d
# Interactive shell experience. Agents run non-interactively; the worker's shell
# is bash from the base image, and the two things dot_zshenv actually did -- mise
# activation and a PATH entry -- are ENV lines in the Containerfile.
.config/zsh
.zshenv
# tmux was only ever here because the instruction core mandated it; that rule was
# removed in #414 and .ci/test-agent-instructions.sh now bans the wording.
.config/tmux
# tokscale's auth step is in 10-auth, already skipped above, so the config has
# nothing to authenticate against.
.config/tokscale
# The agent set in a worker is Claude Code and Codex; agy is excluded with it.
.gemini
